You mentioned to me a JSON output file, but I wanted to explain how I'm
using your script, which may inform future changes.  What I'm looking to
do is not just see how everyone is doing, but I want to look at point
history so I can see /where/ people got their points.  For example, if
someone is sitting on 32 points, I want to see that their history is
5/8/23/24/29/31/32.
Using this data I see the deltas are:  5/3/15/1/5/2/1
Using THIS data (and associated dates), I can surmise that they got 15
from Pittsburg, and then have 5/3/1/5/2 from As and Bs, and the +1 at the
end is most likely a 3rd at an A (2 points) that replace the 3rd at a B (1
point).

THIS is what I want the script to be able to help me compute.  The way I
do it now is to download each update into its own webcache.  Then I can
do:

for cachedir in webcache/2* ; do ./atastandings -C $cachedir -T -B -m
division -c B01B -d .... --competition combat-weapons -s <name> |& grep -i
<person city> ; done) | awk '{print $NF,$0}' | sort -nu | cut -f2- -d' '

This will give me the personal history over time for this competitor.



I've also used the script to go beyond world-top-10.  What I do is search
through all the districts and then sort the results for, e.g., the top 20:

./atastandings -C ~/ATA/2024-2025/.webcache -c B01B -B -m division -d
mid-america -d midwest -d north -d northeast -d northwest -d southeast -d
south -d southwest -d rockies -T --competition combat-weapons | grep -v
"DISTRICT STANDINGS FOR" | grep -v '^$' | awk '{print $NF,$0}' | sort -nr
| cut -f2- -d' ' | head -20


Then I know who to search for in more detailed form.

You'll notice that here I do sort -nr because I want the highest scores at
the front.  In the previous run I use sort -nu because I want to start
from the lowest score, and I only want to see the changes.

I can't think of an easy way to do the history with the script, but the
ability to combine multiple districts and sorting is something that would
be useful.
